  "slug": "optical-fidelity-dimensional-3797",
  "summary": "Four November 2026 preprints report multidimensional optical encoding, sensing and wavefront control, with quantitative accuracy and delivery figures.",
  "synthesis": "The shared thread is the use of multiple optical degrees of freedom, including spatial, temporal, spectral and polarization information, to encode or recover information beyond simple intensity. One preprint reconstructs 2D and 3D fiber shape from a single end using picosecond time-resolved single-photon returns on a 32x32 SPAD array. Another stores data in polarized light within laser-induced nanostructures, reporting 99.45% raw accuracy. A third uses gradient metasurface pixels to convert spectral and polarization content into spatial patterns for snapshot infrared sensing. The fourth applies a phase-only MEMS modulator with 8,192 elements at 250 kHz refresh to wavefront shaping through multimode fiber, reporting 70% light delivery. The applications differ, so the dominant thread is multidimensional optical readout and control rather than a single method. The new element across the set is the pairing of single-photon time resolution, fast phase modulation and pixelated polarization-spectral encoding.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
